== Fixed-AMI variant (Aclara I-210+c / NIC 511) ==

I spent some time reverse-engineering the mesh radio on my own power meter — an
Aclara I-210+c with a Silver Spring NIC 511 module, on a fixed AMI network. It's
passive receive only, with an RTL-SDR. The transport layer came apart cleanly
(framing, whitening, CRC, addressing, channel plan, hopping); the actual meter
payload is AES-CCM* encrypted, so that stays closed.

The important thing up front: '''this is not the same on-air format as the other
Silver Spring work.''' There are at least three different framings floating
around and they don't share a scrambler or a sync:

* This wiki page's own capture (meter <code>…008C900A</code>) has the
  post-preamble <code>de 9d 27 27 16 66 f0 6c</code> — matches neither of the
  others.
* rtl_433's <code>silver_spring_mesh</code> decoder (Benjamin Larsson) handles a
  drive-by/water-meter variant: <code>SFD 0xF3A0</code>, 3-byte PHR, an '''8-bit'''
  scrambler, a 4-byte CRC-32/MPEG-2 FCS.
* What I'm describing here (fixed-AMI NIC 511): sync <code>0C 5F &lt;ch&gt; FF</code>,
  2-byte inverted PHR, a '''degree-9''' LFSR whitening, and a 1-byte
  CRC-32-derived trailer.

I checked, and the two scramblers really are different — Larsson's 8-bit one
descrambles none of my frames at any of its 255 seeds, and Berlekamp–Massey puts
my keystream's linear complexity at 9, which an 8-bit LFSR can't reach. So the
rest of this is just the NIC 511 variant; go to rtl_433 for the water one.

Everything below is reproducible — code and a longer write-up are at
[https://github.com/bash-tilde/silverspring-ami-rf github.com/bash-tilde/silverspring-ami-rf].

=== The PHY, and the channel is in the clear ===

2-GFSK, h≈0.5, running at 100, 150 or 200 kBd depending on the frame. What looks
like a sync word is actually <code>0C 5F &lt;X&gt; FF</code> — and byte 2 is the
channel number, as <code>N = 255 - X</code>. Fit the carrier against that and you
get a clean grid:

 f(N) = 902.2990 MHz + 299.991 kHz * N        N = 0..86   (87 channels)

Residuals are under 2 kHz on a 300 kHz grid. Note that's 87 channels at 300 kHz,
not the 64-at-400-kHz from the FCC filing — could be a firmware/config
difference, my unit is a NIC 511.

=== Whitening: it's a degree-9 LFSR ===

The PSDU is whitened. The trick to cracking it: the long frames have a big run of
all-zero payload, so on the air that region is just the raw keystream. Run
Berlekamp–Massey on it and you get linear complexity 9 and the polynomial falls
right out:

 b[n] = b[n-1] ^ b[n-2] ^ b[n-5] ^ b[n-8] ^ b[n-9]        period 255

You don't even need to know the per-frame seed. Plaintext bytes 0-4 are always
the serial prefix <code>00 13 50 05 00</code>, so <code>mask[0:5] = cipher[0:5] ^
prefix</code> gives you the first 40 keystream bits, and the recurrence gives you
the rest. Every frame I caught de-whitens to that prefix.

=== The trailing byte is a CRC-32 ===

This page currently says "no checking is done of the packets" — there is a check,
it's just at the end. The last byte is the top byte of a CRC-32 (poly
<code>0x04C11DB7</code>, MSB-first, init 0) over the de-whitened frame, XORed with
a per-length constant:

 K = {9:0x78, 12:0x0E, 14:0x5B, 16:0x7A, 21:0xEB, 22:0xE8, 29:0x05,
      55:0xA6, 111:0x7D, 112:0x2D, 134:0x6E, 213:0x60}

That validates on ~98% of captured frames; the handful that fail are corrupt
(bit errors show up as junk in the known-zero padding).

=== Frame layout ===

The length tells you the header stack. This accounts for every byte:

 frame := SRC(8) [DST(8)] {headers} payload TRAILER(1)

 ln= 12   SRC | 04 81 xx | TRAILER
 ln= 14   SRC | 01 03 aa bb cc | TRAILER
 ln= 22   SRC | DST | 01 03 aa bb cc | TRAILER
 ln= 29   SRC | DST | 01 03 .. | PAYLOAD(7) | TRAILER
 ln= 55   SRC | 01 03 .. | 04 81 xx | PAYLOAD(38) | TRAILER
 ln=111   SRC | 01 80 | 00 82 .. | PAYLOAD(96) | TRAILER   (beacon)
 ln=213   SRC | 00 82 .. | 01 03 .. | PAYLOAD(195) | TRAILER  (encrypted)

Addresses are 8 bytes, OUI <code>00:13:50</code>. Endpoints are serial-form
(<code>00:13:50:05:00:xx…</code>), access points are EUI-64
(<code>00:13:50:FF:FE:60:xx…</code>). The second byte of the PHR turns out to be
<code>base(channel) XOR frame-type</code>, where the type is 0=broadcast,
2=unicast, 3=beacon.

=== Beacon timing and hopping ===

One node (a relay) beacons on a dead-steady 120.000 s clock — phase holds to a
quarter-second over hundreds of cycles. Number the beacons by slot and the
channel is just <code>slot mod 83</code>. I predicted a bunch of hops forward and
got 153 out of 153 right. The cycle is 83 x 120 s, about 2h46m.

=== The payload is encrypted ===

The <code>ln=213</code> frames are the odd ones out — 195 bytes of uniform,
zero-free, incompressible payload, where every other class is full of structure
and zeros. Berlekamp–Massey gives it full linear complexity, so it's not a linear
scrambler like the rest. It's AES-CCM* (the usual Itron/Certicom Suite B setup).
The key lives in the head-end key manager and the meter's secure element, not on
the air — so this is where it stops. The transport is wide open; the meter
reading isn't coming out over the radio.

